1.

Strategy of Clever Player - 
A. Start from the first row of the board.
B. Attempt to place a mark in the first block (leftmost column) of the current row.
C. If the block is occupied, move to the next block in the same row.
D. If the entire row is occupied, proceed to the next row.
 E. Continue this process row by row until a block is found or all blocks are checked.
 
Strategy of Genius Player - 
A. Start from the second column of the board.
B. Attempt to place a mark in the first block (top row) of the current column.
C. If the block is occupied, move to the next block in the same column.
D. If the entire column is occupied, proceed to the next column.
E. Continue this process column by column until a block is found
or all the blocks from the second column to the last one are checked.
F. If all from the second column to the last one are occupied,
 start checking the first column and follow the same logic.
G. Continue this process until a block is found or all blocks are checked.

2.

The advantages of making a code where each of the players implement a shared interface are:

A. Polymorphism - there is an interface for the rendering classes and also for the players classes,
   what makes the option to perform a single task in different ways.

B. Encapsulation - Each class objects are responsible for their own class and there is
   no overlap between classes responsibilities (no class uses other classes objects).
   Also, we used access modifiers for the methods and objects to preserve encapsulation.

C. Abstraction - The player just writes the arguments to the game and it starts.
   He doesn't need to understand the code to start a game.
   The main function is very simple and leaves out the details of "how" the program is run.
   Even Adding / Removing a type of player is much easier,
   you just need to change one line of code in PlayerFactory.
   This shows that the code is abstract.

